Nice — let’s cut the fluff and get you exam-ready. I’ll explain the theory of inheritance in C++ (simple, precise), then give you full, runnable example code for each common form (single, multiple, multilevel, hierarchical, hybrid) and for the three inheritance access modes (public/protected/private) so you can see exactly what changes. At the end I’ll list the image files you uploaded (so you can open them if you want the slides).

---

# Quick theory — the bits you must remember
- **Inheritance**: a derived class reuses/extends a base class. Syntax:
```cpp
class Derived : access-specifier Base { /*...*/ };
```
- **Access specifiers inside a class**: `public`, `protected`, `private` control member visibility.
  - `public` members → accessible everywhere.
  - `protected` members → accessible in derived classes, not to outside code.
  - `private` members → accessible only inside the class itself (not in derived).
- **Mode of inheritance** (the `access-specifier` before the base):
  - `public` inheritance: public → public, protected → protected.
  - `protected` inheritance: public → protected, protected → protected.
  - `private` inheritance: public → private, protected → private.
- **Forms of inheritance**:
  - **Single**: one base, one derived.
  - **Multiple**: one derived, multiple base classes.
  - **Multilevel**: chain A → B → C.
  - **Hierarchical**: one base, multiple derived classes.
  - **Hybrid**: mix of above (e.g., multiple + multilevel).

---

# 1) Single inheritance — simple, clear
Theory: derived class inherits members from one base class.

Full runnable code:
```cpp
// single_inheritance.cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Account {
public:
    float salary;
    void showSalary() const {
        cout << "Salary: " << salary << endl;
    }
};

class Programmer : public Account { // public inheritance
public:
    float bonus;
    void showBonus() const {
        cout << "Bonus: " << bonus << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Programmer p;
    p.salary = 60000;
    p.bonus = 5000;
    p.showSalary();
    p.showBonus();
    return 0;
}
```
Expected output:
```
Salary: 60000
Bonus: 5000
```
Explanation: `Programmer` directly inherits `salary` and `showSalary` from `Account`.

---

# 2) Multiple inheritance — combine multiple bases
Theory: derived class gets members from more than one base class. Watch for name collisions.

Full runnable code:
```cpp
// multiple_inheritance.cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Engine {
public:
    void start() const { cout << "Engine started\n"; }
};

class Wheels {
public:
    void roll() const { cout << "Wheels rolling\n"; }
};

class Car : public Engine, public Wheels {
public:
    void drive() const {
        start();  // from Engine
        roll();   // from Wheels
        cout << "Car is driving\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Car c;
    c.drive();
    return 0;
}
```
Expected output:
```
Engine started
Wheels rolling
Car is driving
```
Note: If both base classes had a method with same name, you'd need qualification (e.g., `Engine::foo()`).

---

# 3) Multilevel inheritance — chain inheritance
Theory: Derived2 inherits from Derived1 which inherits from Base.

Full runnable code:
```cpp
// multilevel_inheritance.cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Student {
protected:
    int roll;
public:
    void setRoll(int r) { roll = r; }
    void showRoll() const { cout << "Roll no: " << roll << "\n"; }
};

class Test : public Student { // first level derivation
protected:
    float marks1, marks2;
public:
    void setMarks(float a, float b) { marks1 = a; marks2 = b; }
    void showMarks() const {
        cout << "Marks in sub1 = " << marks1 << "\n";
        cout << "Marks in sub2 = " << marks2 << "\n";
    }
};

class Result : public Test { // second level derivation
public:
    void showResult() {
        float total = marks1 + marks2; // allowed: marks1/marks2 are protected in Test
        showRoll();    // from Student
        showMarks();   // from Test
        cout << "Total marks = " << total << "\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Result r;
    r.setRoll(111);
    r.setMarks(75.0f, 59.5f);
    r.showResult();
    return 0;
}
```
Expected output:
```
Roll no: 111
Marks in sub1 = 75
Marks in sub2 = 59.5
Total marks = 134.5
```
Point: `Result` can access `protected` members of `Test`/`Student`.

---

# 4) Hierarchical inheritance — one base, many derived
Theory: Several classes derive from same base.

Full runnable code:
```cpp
// hierarchical_inheritance.cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Animal {
public:
    void eat() const { cout << "Animal eats\n"; }
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    void bark() const { cout << "Dog barks\n"; }
};

class Cat : public Animal {
public:
    void meow() const { cout << "Cat meows\n"; }
};

int main() {
    Dog d;
    Cat c;
    d.eat(); d.bark();
    c.eat(); c.meow();
    return 0;
}
```
Expected output:
```
Animal eats
Dog barks
Animal eats
Cat meows
```
Each derived class shares the same base functionality.

---

# 5) Hybrid inheritance — mixed forms (example: multiple + multilevel)
Theory: any mix. Be cautious about the **diamond problem** when same ancestor appears twice — solve with `virtual` inheritance.

Example that demonstrates diamond and how to fix with `virtual`:
```cpp
// hybrid_inheritance.cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Base
class A {
public:
    void showA() const { cout << "A::showA\n"; }
};

// B and C both inherit A
class B : virtual public A {     // virtual inheritance
};

class C : virtual public A {     // virtual inheritance
};

// D inherits B and C -> diamond
class D : public B, public C {
public:
    void showD() const { cout << "D::showD\n"; }
};

int main() {
    D obj;
    obj.showA(); // only one A subobject exists because of virtual inheritance
    obj.showD();
    return 0;
}
```
Expected output:
```
A::showA
D::showD
```
If `B` and `C` were not `virtual` inheriting `A`, `D` would contain two separate `A` subobjects and `obj.showA()` would be ambiguous.

---

# 6) Modes of inheritance & access effects — short demo
Theory recap (again): inheritance mode changes how base class `public`/`protected` map in derived class.

Demo code showing mapping:
```cpp
// inheritance_modes.cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Base {
public:
    int pub = 1;
protected:
    int prot = 2;
private:
    int priv = 3;
};

class PublicDerived : public Base {
public:
    void print() {
        cout << "PublicDerived: pub=" << pub << ", prot=" << prot << "\n";
        // priv is NOT accessible here
    }
};

class ProtectedDerived : protected Base {
public:
    void print() {
        cout << "ProtectedDerived: pub(now protected)=" << pub << ", prot=" << prot << "\n";
    }
};

class PrivateDerived : private Base {
public:
    void print() {
        cout << "PrivateDerived: pub(now private)=" << pub << ", prot=" << prot << "\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    PublicDerived pd;
    pd.print();
    cout << "pd.pub accessible from main? "; 
    // pd.pub; // ERROR: pub remains public in PublicDerived, so it is accessible => uncomment to test.
    ProtectedDerived protD;
    protD.print();
    // protD.pub; // ERROR: pub became protected in ProtectedDerived
    PrivateDerived privD;
    privD.print();
    // privD.pub; // ERROR: pub became private in PrivateDerived
    return 0;
}
```
Notes:
- `Base::priv` is never accessible in derived classes.
- Public inheritance preserves interface (is-a relation).
- Protected/private inheritance hide base interface from outside.

---

# Tips for exam / debugging
- If you get ambiguous call errors in multiple inheritance: qualify with `Base::method()`.
- Diamond inheritance needs `virtual` base to have a single copy of the base subobject.
- Use `protected` for "internal but available to derived classes". Use `private` to hide implementation.

